> **Simulation convention (no real React here):** the grader has no React or DOM.
> A "component" is a **pure function** `(props) => Element` where an element is the plain object
> `{ type: string, props: object, children: array }` (children: strings or nested elements).
> Exercises are **TypeScript**: your code is transpiled (types are erased, not checked) and the tests
> verify runtime behavior — but the starters give you the real type signatures, and writing the types
> is the point: they make the correct implementation the natural one.


## Components that hold other components

Three composition styles cover almost everything:

**1. Children** — the component wraps whatever it is given:

```ts
type Child = Element | string;

interface CardProps { title: string; children: Child[] }

function Card({ title, children }: CardProps): Element {
  return {
    type: 'div',
    props: { className: 'card' },
    children: [
      { type: 'h3', props: {}, children: [title] },
      { type: 'div', props: { className: 'card-body' }, children },
    ],
  };
}
```

The body div receives the children ARRAY as-is — Card positions them without inspecting them.

**2. Slots** — multiple named children: `{ header: Element, footer: Element }`. Use when "the children" is not one blob but distinct regions.

**3. Render props** — the parent provides a FUNCTION that produces elements:

```ts
interface ListProps<T> {
  items: T[];
  renderItem: (item: T) => Element;
}
```

`List` owns the looping and the `<ul>`/`<li>` scaffolding; the caller owns what each row looks like. The generic `<T>` ties the two props together: whatever type `items` holds, `renderItem` must accept.

### Common mistakes

- Typing children as `any[]` — you lose every guarantee; use `(Element | string)[]`.
- A wrapper that clones/rebuilds its children — pass the references through untouched.
- Render props typed `(item: any) => any`, which silently accepts a renderItem for the wrong row type.

> **Simulation convention (no real React here):** the grader has no React or DOM.
> A "component" is a **pure function** `(props) => Element` where an element is the plain object
> `{ type: string, props: object, children: array }` (children: strings or nested elements).
> Exercises are **TypeScript**: your code is transpiled (types are erased, not checked) and the tests
> verify runtime behavior — but the starters give you the real type signatures, and writing the types
> is the point: they make the correct implementation the natural one.


## Handlers are typed contracts

A callback prop's type says exactly what the child will report:

```ts
interface SearchBoxProps {
  value: string;
  onChange: (value: string) => void;   // the child reports the NEW TEXT, not an event
  onSubmit: (value: string) => void;
}
```

Passing the extracted **value** instead of the raw event is a deliberate API choice: parents get what they care about, and only the leaf component knows event plumbing. In this course a simulated change event is:

```ts
interface ChangeEvent { target: { value: string } }
interface CheckEvent  { target: { checked: boolean } }
interface KeyEvent    { key: string }
```

### Worked example: the extraction boundary

```ts
function SearchBox({ value, onChange }: SearchBoxProps): Element {
  return {
    type: 'input',
    props: {
      value,
      onInput: (e: ChangeEvent) => onChange(e.target.value),  // unwrap HERE
    },
    children: [],
  };
}
```

The arrow adapts between two signatures: the DOM-ish `(e: ChangeEvent) => void` that inputs emit, and the clean `(value: string) => void` the parent wants. That adapter line is the whole job of a controlled input.

Handlers also **compose**: "call the prop handler, then also track analytics" is a function that calls two functions. You will write the composer.

### Common mistakes

- `onChange: Function` — accepts anything, guarantees nothing. Always write the full signature.
- Forwarding the raw event upward — every parent now depends on the event shape.
- Calling the handler instead of passing it: `onClick: handleClick()` fires during render.

> **Simulation convention (no real React here):** the grader has no React or DOM.
> A "component" is a **pure function** `(props) => Element` where an element is the plain object
> `{ type: string, props: object, children: array }` (children: strings or nested elements).
