## Actions as a discriminated union

Type the WHOLE action vocabulary as one union; `type` is the discriminant:

```ts
type TodoAction =
  | { type: 'add'; text: string }
  | { type: 'toggle'; id: number }
  | { type: 'remove'; id: number }
  | { type: 'clearCompleted' };

function todoReducer(state: Todo[], action: TodoAction): Todo[] {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'add':    return [...state, { id: nextId(state), text: action.text, done: false }];
    case 'toggle': return state.map(t => t.id === action.id ? { ...t, done: !t.done } : t);
    case 'remove': return state.filter(t => t.id !== action.id);
    case 'clearCompleted': return state.filter(t => !t.done);
  }
}
```

Inside `case 'add'`, TypeScript has NARROWED the action: `action.text` exists, `action.id` does not — touching the wrong payload is a compile error. That is the whole payoff of the union: every case sees exactly its own payload.

### Exhaustiveness

With a typed union, a switch that handles every member needs no default — and you can make the compiler ENFORCE completeness:

```ts
default: {
  const _exhaustive: never = action;   // if a new action type is added and unhandled,
  return state;                        // this line stops compiling
}
```

`never` is the type with no values. If all cases are handled, `action` in the default is `never` and the assignment compiles; add a fifth action without a case, and `action` still has that type there — compile error at the reducer, not a bug report from production.

### Common mistakes

- `action: { type: string; payload?: any }` — the "flexible" shape that checks nothing.
- Payloads carrying the computed next state instead of event facts.
- Forgetting that at RUNTIME an unknown action can still arrive (from a typo in JS, a replay log): return state, don't throw.

> **Simulation convention (no real React here):** the grader has no React or DOM.
> A "component" is a **pure function** `(props) => Element` where an element is the plain object
> `{ type: string, props: object, children: array }` (children: strings or nested elements).
> Exercises are **TypeScript**: your code is transpiled (types are erased, not checked) and the tests
> verify runtime behavior — but the starters give you the real type signatures, and writing the types
> is the point: they make the correct implementation the natural one.


## The wire shape is not your shape

What the API sends (the **DTO** — data transfer object) is optimized for the server: snake_case, nullable everything, nested envelopes. Components deserve a **view model**: the fields the UI needs, in UI shapes, with nulls resolved. A **mapper** converts at the boundary:

```ts
interface UserDto {
  id: number;
  first_name: string;
  last_name: string | null;
  avatar_url: string | null;
}

interface UserVM {
  id: number;
  fullName: string;        // built, trimmed
  avatarUrl: string;       // null resolved to a default
}

function toUserVM(dto: UserDto): UserVM {
  return {
    id: dto.id,
    fullName: (dto.first_name + ' ' + (dto.last_name ?? '')).trim(),
    avatarUrl: dto.avatar_url ?? '/img/default-avatar.png',
  };
}
```

Every `??` is a decision made ONCE, at the boundary — no component ever writes `user.avatar_url ?? default` again.

## Envelopes and normalization

Paginated APIs wrap data: `Page<T> = { items: T[]; page: number; totalPages: number }`. Generic helpers (`mapPage`, `emptyPage`) work for every T.

Lists keyed by id beat arrays for lookup-heavy state. The **normalized** shape is:

```ts
{ byId: Record<string, T>, allIds: string[] }   // allIds preserves order
```

### Common mistakes

- Spreading DTO fields into state "for later" — snake_case leaks everywhere, and nobody knows which fields are trusted.
- `||` instead of `??` (a 0 or '' from the API becomes your default).
- Normalizing but forgetting `allIds` — objects don't promise ordering; the array does.

